Aerie-Purpose: Give the page its room in one step when the Aerie sidebar hides, while the column slides out over it.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: chrome/browser/ui/views/frame (vertical_tab_strip_region_view.*)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Issue #215 (item 5 of the rest of #177). The page card does not follow the column with a transform: it takes its new bounds in one
step and only the column animates. A card transform cannot move the page. The page's native view is a layer of the window, not of the
card, and NativeViewHost::Layout places it in window coordinates with the card's layer transform at that moment folded in; the layout that
follows a bounds change ran after the transform started, so the page stayed one column width off (right after a hide, left after a show).

A show already gave the page its room in one step. A hide used to hold the room until the slide had ended; now it gives it at once and
the column slides out over the room the card is taking. The state and the numbers are in the overlay
(aerie_sidebar::HideSlide, components/aerie_sidebar/aerie_sidebar_motion.h). While the slide runs the region's preferred and minimum
width are 0, so the page has its room, and the region floats at the column's own width over that room (aerie_sidebar::kFloatingWidth,
the property the auto-hide peek uses; issue #237). A zero-width region with its content hanging outside left the compositor one frame
of the slide (the Bench's sidebar_frames check measured 1 frame in 12), because the region had no bounds to draw. When the slide
ends the column leaves the layout as before.

The show and the hide also start at the next begin-frame, not inside the key press (aerie_sidebar::AerieFrameStart), and a drag of the
resize edge applies once per begin-frame, not once per pointer move. A key press lands anywhere in a vsync, and the layout, paint and
raster it causes then run from that moment: the first frame was on screen 15 to 25 ms after its begin-frame, against a 16.7 ms budget.
Pref changes that come in before the frame are read together, so two Ctrl+S in one frame cancel each other.

